=== lupin CHANGELOG 0.1.54 entry
## 0.1.54 — 2026-10-17

THE FIFTY-FOURTH, the lupin half of wolf 0.2.31. **Pin unchanged:
`294d626d` (wolf-lang v0.2.24).** Forty-one commits since 0.1.53: the
lupin halves of two wolf-lang lanes, s241 (the group send; 5 commits)
and s242 (the job wait; 6 commits), and is76 (lupin catches up with
wolf 0.2.31's s237 and s239 and closes #251, #250, #249, #245; 30
commits, two planted breaks, `e18b7c9` and `0787454`, and their revert,
`21a7e94`, among them). Every lane was merged before the release lane
(r36) began; r36 adds the version.

- **What changes a program's answer here:** a plain shift by 128 or
  more is the value's (it trapped), a set bit shifted past every bit
  this machine holds traps (it printed 0), a module `const` whose plain
  shift leaves its type is E0706 (it printed the wide value), and the
  net family sets `os_error()` (it stayed 0). A run that ends in this
  machine's own refusal now ends the processes it started (#249).
- **The other lanes in wolf 0.2.31 have no lupin half of their own.**
  wolf-lang's s239 (the readiness set before `main`) and s243 (the
  signal hub before `main`) are the compiled runtime's start; lupin has
  no reactor and no signal family, and is76 mirrors s239's
  `os_error()`. s238 and s240 change no program (a test race; a note).
- **The walk at this release:** 1002 files, 955 entries, 47 members;
  760 reach `run`, 721 match, 94 dynamic, 64 conservatism, 72 out of
  scope, 4 mismatch, every one filed. Unchanged from 0.1.53, entry for
  entry (the pin did not move; s241's, s242's and is76's rows live in
  `tests/` until a re-pin brings them into the walk; is76 checked every
  vendored file's verdict, phase and stdout digest unchanged).
- **Rulings now made** that the lanes implemented ahead of them, each as
  implemented: #79 (s241's R1–R9; R8: lupin answers the shape rows and
  refuses each well-formed call by name), #84 (s242's R1–R9; R9: lupin
  answers `(-1, 0)` with no live child and refuses the poll by name
  once one is live) and #77 A (the shift rule, which is76 mirrors).
- **A row that now finishes:** wolf-std's
  `tests/x/crypto/sha2/wycheproof_hkdf256_full.lu` runs to `exit(0)`
  inside the 50,000,000-step budget, where 0.1.53 answered `unsupported`
  at the budget; wolf-std's ledger records it after this release.
- **lupin's archive still ships no std** (ruling #59 B(1)): a `lupin`
  installed alone answers `unsupported` for `use std.…` with nothing
  set; one placed beside a 0.2.31 `wolf` reads the `std/` wolf ships.

### The mirrors

- **The job wait — the mirror** (wolf-lang s242, #680; `[os.proc.poll]`,
  `[os.proc.wait.note]` R9, ruling #84). `os_wait_poll` resolves with
  its declared row (`{io}`). With no child left — none spawned, or every
  one reaped — it answers `(-1, 0)`, as every compiler machine does,
  decided without the host; with a child not yet reaped it is **refused
  by name**: the answer needs `waitpid` with `WUNTRACED`, std's
  `try_wait` sees an end but never a stop, and this machine admits no
  `unsafe`. The compiler's witnesses that listen for `CHILD` stop earlier,
  at `os_signal_listen`, which lupin never serves. `tests/job_wait_s242.rs`
  (3 tests) holds the compiler's corpus row and three fixtures byte for
  byte.
- **The group send, the move, host numbers and a signal's words — the
  mirror** (wolf-lang s241, #739, #740, #741, #762; ruling #79 R8). The
  six names resolve with their declared rows: `os_signal_send_group` and
  `os_signal_send_all` (`{denied, invalid, io, not_found,
  unsupported}`), `os_set_pgid` (`{denied, invalid, io, unsupported}`),
  `os_signal_last` and `os_signal_realtime` (`{unsupported}`) and
  `os_signal_text` (`{invalid, unsupported}`). Each answers its SHAPE
  rows as every machine (`invalid`: a group not above 1, a group to move
  into below 0, past a pid's width, a meaning that is neither 0, one of
  the 28, nor `-N` with `1 <= N <=` the host's last — 64 on linux, 31 on
  macOS — and a text of 0); windows answers `unsupported` after the shape;
  a well-formed call on linux and macOS is **refused by name** before any
  host call — std has no `kill(-pgid)`, no `setpgid` on itself, no
  `SIGRTMIN` and no `strsignal`, and this machine admits no `unsafe`.
  `os_signal_send` takes `-N` in its shape too (a well-formed one is
  refused by name, as before). `tests/group_s241.rs` (4 tests) holds the
  compiler's corpus row byte for byte and its fixtures' refusals.

- **lupin catches up** (is76; wolf-interp#251, #250, #249, #245).
  - **Shifts, `[type.int.shift]`** (wolf-lang s237, ruling #77 A; #251).
    A count of 128 or more on a plain integer is the value's shift, not
    a trap (`255 >> 200` at `u8` is 0; 0.1.53 ran out of `i128` shift
    range), and a set bit shifted past every bit this machine holds
    traps (`2 << 127` at `u64` printed 0). A module `const` or `let`
    with a plain integer type is folded by the rule at resolve, so
    `const K: u8 = 200 << 1` is E0706 at the operator, the compiler's
    span (it printed 400); the fold reads literals, `-`, groups, the
    arithmetic and bitwise operators and other such items of the same
    type, and says nothing of any other shape. A literal-only term meets
    its declared integer type before its operators run — a binding's
    annotation, a module item's, an integer place assigned or
    compound-assigned, a function's tail and `return` — so `let r:
    wrapping[u8] = 1 << 9` is 2 and `255 >> 8` there is 255 (both
    computed wide and trapped); an argument is not yet such a context.
    A shift whose two operands' declared types differ (`x << n`, `x:
    u64`, `n: int`) is E0401 at the count where both are parameters the
    body never rebinds, or `as` casts; an inferred binding says nothing.
    Every vendored corpus file answers as at 3cc3f9c (1002 files;
    verdict, phase and stdout digest, release builds, kasumi).
    `tests/shift_is76.rs` holds wolf-lang's six shift rows byte for byte.
  - **The net family sets `os_error()`, `[os.net.error]`** (wolf-lang
    s239, #751; ruling #80; #250): cleared on entry, the host's number
    where the host refused (ECONNREFUSED, EADDRINUSE, ENOENT, EPIPE,
    EMFILE at the descriptor limit), 0 after a success, a failure
    decided before the host, a fired budget and an end of stream. A
    unix listen on an existing path now asks the bind (EADDRINUSE beside
    `exists`) rather than look at the path first. Every number in
    `tests/net_error_is76.rs` is the checked machine's on linux.
  - **A refused run ends what it started** (#249). When the run ends in
    this machine's own refusal (`unsupported`), every process the
    program started and had not reaped is ended — a job that leads its
    process group with the group, through `sh`'s `kill -s KILL --
    -PGID` (std has no `kill(2)`; no `unsafe`), any other child by
    `Child::kill` — and reaped. The record adds `x-ended` (their ids)
    and `x-unsupported-at: "run"`; `phase_reached` stays `resolve`, the
    deepest phase completed (`[proto.record.phase]`; the checked
    machine answers `mem` for its own run-time refusal). An exit or a
    trap leaves children alone, as a native process's are.
  - **The wasm decline** (#245): `os_pid`, `os_ppid`,
    `os_signal_number` and the rest of the tier are declined as the
    hosted-only process and signal tier with no host process in a
    page, not as "the s40 process trio" with "no processes to spawn".

