## corpus/comptime/shift_value.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="8 0 0 -1 128 2 255\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: type.int.shift, comptime.arith
//!
//! s237 (`[type.int.shift]`): a constant shift is the run time's. A
//! plain count at or past the width answers the value's floor or
//! product (`255 >> 8` at `u8` is 0, `0 << 8` is 0, `-8 >> 64` is -1),
//! and a `wrapping[T]` count masks to the width (`1 << 9` at
//! `wrapping[u8]` is 2). Until s237 the constant evaluator refused
//! every count at or past the width (E0706, "shift by 8 exceeds the
//! width") where the compiled program computed an answer.
## corpus/faults/int_shift_count_negative.lu
//! check: run(exit=trap(overflow))
//! phase: run
//! conforms: type.int.shift
//!
//! s237 (`[type.int.shift]`): a negative count has no value to answer
//! with (`8 >> -1` is not `8 << 1`): the `overflow` trap, for `>>` and
//! `<<` alike. At 0.2.29 native and release masked the count to 63 and
//! printed 0; lupin 0.1.52 trapped.
## corpus/faults/int_shift_left_overflow.lu
//! check: run(exit=trap(overflow))
//! phase: run
//! conforms: type.int.shift
//!
//! s237 (wolf-lang#736, `[type.int.shift]`): `255 << 1` at `u8` is 510,
//! which leaves the type: the `overflow` trap, as `255 * 2` is. At
