diff --git a/book/ch03.md b/book/ch03.md
index 02f1f40..819980f 100644
--- a/book/ch03.md
+++ b/book/ch03.md
@@ -571,6 +571,54 @@ of `checksum += 1` can look up what that line meant. A hash function
 wants `wrapping`. A gauge wants `saturating`. A receipt total wants
 neither, which is why the trap above is the right answer.
 
+A shift is arithmetic too, and it keeps the same rule. On a plain
+integer type, `x << n` is `x` times two to the `n` and `x >> n` is `x`
+divided by two to the `n`, rounded down, both computed on the values, so
+a `<<` that leaves the type traps exactly as `*` does:
+
+```wolf,run(exit=trap(overflow))
+fn main() -> !int {
+    let flags: u8 = 200
+    print("{flags >> 1} {flags >> 8}")
+    print("{flags << 1}")
+    0
+}
+```
+
+```console
+$ lupin shift.lu
+100 0
+shift.lu: trap(overflow): `<<` produced 400, outside `u8` — checked arithmetic traps in every profile (X3); spell intended overflow `wrapping[u8]` [arith.checked] at 4:13
+$ echo $?
+3
+```
+
+The first line is the half of the rule that never traps. `>>` rounds
+toward negative infinity, so it cannot leave the type, and a count at or
+past the width is not a case of its own: every bit has gone, and `200 >>
+8` at `u8` is 0. The second line is the half that does. 400 does not fit
+in eight bits, and a bit shifted off the top is a value lost, which is
+what an overflow is. A negative count traps as well, for either operator.
+Both operands of a shift have one type, so the count here is a `u8` too,
+and a literal count takes the type of the value it shifts.
+
+The shift a processor does, which drops what falls off and takes the
+count modulo the width, is `wrapping[T]`'s, for code that wants bits
+rather than a number:
+
+```wolf,run(exit=0, stdout="144 1 200")
+fn main() -> !int {
+    let flags: wrapping[u8] = 200
+    print("{flags << 1} {flags >> 7} {flags << 8}")
+    0
+}
+```
+
+`200 << 1` drops the top bit and keeps 144, `>> 7` keeps the one bit
+left, and `<< 8` shifts by nothing at all, because 8 modulo the width is
+0. A hash, a checksum or a set of flags packed into a word wants this. A
+quantity never does.
+
 > **The bill for checking.** Every arithmetic operation in wolf carries
 > a test the C version does not: compute, check the flag the hardware
 > already set, branch to the trap on failure. The branch is never taken
